        "file_path": "/workspace/REFACTOR_PLAN.md",
        "content": "# Refactor Plan: Propagating a New `DecoratorMetadata` AST Node from `@babel/parser`\n\n## 1. Execution Order\n\n1. **`babel`** \u2014 implement the `DecoratorMetadata` node in `@babel/parser` (and the\n   corresponding `@babel/types` definition, `@babel/traverse` visitor keys, and\n   `@babel/generator` codegen so the node round-trips).\n2. **`next.js`** \u2014 bump the vendored/pinned Babel version and update any\n   Babel-AST-consuming build code that does exhaustive node-type handling.\n3. **`webpack`** \u2014 **no update required**. See \u00a72 for why it is not actually on\n   the propagation path.\n\nThere is no parallelism available between steps 1 and 2: next.js cannot safely\nadopt the new node type until babel has shipped it (real version edge, not\njust a dev/test edge \u2014 see \u00a72). Step 3 is dropped from the plan entirely.\n\n## 2. Dependency Graph\n\n```\nbabel (@babel/parser, @babel/core, @babel/traverse, @babel/types, @babel/generator)\n   \u2502\n   \u2502  packages/next/package.json \u2192 devDependencies:\n   \u2502    \"@babel/core\": \"7.18.0\"\n   \u2502    \"@babel/traverse\": \"7.18.0\"\n   \u2502    \"@babel/types\": \"7.18.0\"\n   \u2502    \"@babel/generator\": \"7.18.0\"\n   \u2502    \"@babel/preset-env\" / \"@babel/preset-react\" / \"@babel/preset-typescript\"\n   \u2502    \"@babel/plugin-proposal-class-properties\", \"@babel/plugin-transform-runtime\", etc.\n   \u25bc\nnext.js\n```\n\n- **babel \u2192 next.js is a real edge**, but it is *not* a live npm `dependencies`\n  edge \u2014 it is a **devDependency + vendoring** edge. Next.js's own build\n  (`taskfile-ncc.js` / `taskfile-swc.js`, driven by `packages/next/package.json`\n  `scripts.build` \u2192 `taskr release`) compiles these Babel packages into\n  `next/dist/compiled/babel/*` and ships that compiled copy inside the\n  published `next` package. Next.js's own source never imports the bare\n  `@babel/core` specifier \u2014 it imports the vendored copy, e.g.\n  `packages/next/src/build/babel/preset.ts`:\n  ```ts\n  import { PluginItem } from 'next/dist/compiled/babel/core'\n  ...\n  presets: [[require('next/dist/compiled/babel/preset-env'), presetEnvConfig], ...]\n  ```\n  and `packages/next/src/build/babel/plugins/react-loadable-plugin.ts`:\n  ```ts\n  import { NodePath, PluginObj, types as BabelTypes } from 'next/dist/compiled/babel/core'\n  ```\n  Consequence: a change to `@babel/parser`'s emitted AST does **not**\n  automatically reach Next.js users. It only propagates once Next.js (a)\n  bumps the pinned `@babel/*` devDependency versions in\n  `packages/next/package.json` and (b) re-runs its `ncc`/vendoring build to\n  regenerate `next/dist/compiled/babel/*`.\n  This vendoring path is exercised by `next/babel` (the preset used by\n  `next-babel-loader` for Pages Router / custom `.babelrc` users) \u2014 confirmed\n  by `packages/next/src/build/babel/preset.ts` and its plugin set\n  (`plugins/react-loadable-plugin`, `plugins/jsx-pragma`,\n  `plugins/optimize-hook-destructuring`, `plugins/amp-attributes`), all of\n  which use Babel's typed visitor API (`NodePath`, `types as BabelTypes`) and\n  would need updating if they do exhaustive node-type switches that must\n  account for `DecoratorMetadata`.\n\n- **babel \u2192 webpack is NOT a real edge \u2014 this repo was expected to be\n  affected but isn't.** `webpack/package.json` lists Babel packages, but only\n  under `devDependencies`:\n  ```json\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"@babel/core\": \"^7.21.4\",\n    \"@babel/preset-react\": \"^7.18.6\",\n    \"babel-loader\": \"^8.1.0\",\n    ...\n  }\n  ```\n  There is **no `@babel/*` entry under `dependencies`**. webpack's own\n  runtime JS parser is `acorn` (`\"acorn\": \"^8.7.1\"` under `dependencies`,\n  plus `acorn-import-assertions`), not Babel. A grep of webpack's `lib/`\n  runtime source for any `@babel/*` import returns nothing \u2014 the\n  `@babel/core`/`babel-loader`/`@babel/preset-react` devDependencies are\n  consumed only by webpack's own test fixtures and `examples/` (e.g.\n  `examples/module-federation/webpack.config.js`,\n  `test/Cli.basictest.js`), i.e. webpack testing itself *against* a loader,\n  not webpack shipping Babel-consuming code. `babel-loader` \u2014 the actual\n  bridge package that would call `@babel/core` to parse user source \u2014 is a\n  **separate npm package, not vendored into this repo**. Since babel-loader\n  is out of scope of the three repos in the workspace, and webpack's own\n  source has zero Babel AST consumption, **webpack is not reached by this\n  change and needs no code update.** (If the user later wants\n  `babel-loader` covered, that is a fourth repo, not part of this graph.)\n\n- **Next.js also vendors webpack itself** (`\"webpack\": \"5.86.0\"` under\n  `packages/next/package.json` devDependencies, compiled into\n  `next/dist/compiled/webpack/webpack` and imported in\n  `packages/next/src/build/webpack-config.ts`), but that edge is irrelevant\n  to this change since webpack itself doesn't touch Babel ASTs.\n\n- **Note on Next.js's other compiler**: Next.js ships a second, independent\n  compilation path via `next-swc` (Rust, `packages/next-swc/`), which\n  implements TypeScript `emitDecoratorMetadata` itself\n  (`packages/next/src/build/swc/options.ts`,\n  `packages/next-swc/crates/next-core/src/transform_options.rs`). This path\n  is SWC-native and does not consume `@babel/parser`'s AST at all \u2014 it is\n  unaffected by this change and out of scope.\n\n## 3. Parallelization\n\n- Steps 1 (babel) and 2 (next.js) are **strictly sequential** \u2014 next.js\n  cannot compile against a `DecoratorMetadata` node babel hasn't emitted yet.\n- Within step 2, once the version bump lands, the following can proceed in\n  parallel (all are independent consumers of the vendored Babel AST, none\n  depend on each other):\n  - Auditing/updating `packages/next/src/build/babel/plugins/*` for\n    exhaustive node-type handling.\n  - Regenerating `next/dist/compiled/babel/*` via the `ncc`/taskr build.\n  - Updating `@types/babel__*` devDependency pins\n    (`@types/babel__core`, `@types/babel__generator`, `@types/babel__traverse`,\n    `@types/babel__template`) to match the new babel types.\n- Step 3 (webpack) requires no work and is not on the critical path at all \u2014\n  it can be dropped from scheduling entirely rather than \"run in parallel.\"\n\n## 4. Breaking vs. Compatible Change Annotations\n\n| Step | Change | Classification |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. babel | Add `DecoratorMetadata` AST node type to `@babel/parser`/`@babel/types` | **Compatible** for babel itself if it's a strict AST addition behind the stage-3 decorators flag (existing node types unaffected, only reachable when parsing decorator syntax with the new flag). **Breaking** for any downstream consumer that does an exhaustive `switch`/visitor over all node types (e.g. AST-to-AST transforms, serializers, custom traversal) without a `default` case \u2014 such code will now silently skip or throw on the new node. |\n| 2. next.js | Bump vendored `@babel/*` devDependencies + regenerate `next/dist/compiled/babel/*` | **Compatible** for the majority of `next/babel` users (the preset only forwards to babel's own presets/plugins). **Potentially breaking** for the four custom plugins under `packages/next/src/build/babel/plugins/` if any assumes a closed set of node types under a class/decorator context. Requires re-running Next.js's own vendoring build, not just a `package.json` bump. |\n| 3. webpack | None | N/A \u2014 not reached |\n\n## 5. Risk Assessment\n\n- **babel**: Standard parser-surface risk \u2014 must gate the new node behind the\n  existing decorators plugin/version flag so non-decorator parses are\n  byte-identical. Regression risk concentrated in `@babel/generator`\n  (must print the new node) and `@babel/traverse` (must register correct\n  visitor keys, or traversal silently skips decorator metadata subtrees).\n  Low risk to ship in a minor version if properly flagged.\n- **next.js**: Medium risk. The change is invisible until Next.js (a) bumps\n  the pinned Babel devDependency versions and (b) rebuilds\n  `next/dist/compiled/babel/*` \u2014 an easy step to forget, since it's a\n  vendoring step, not a `package.json`-only bump; a version bump alone with a\n  stale compiled bundle silently no-ops. Additional risk: Next.js pins an\n  *old* Babel minor (`7.18.0`) against the workspace's `7.22.9`+ \u2014 the\n  upgrade itself (independent of the new node) may pull in unrelated\n  breaking changes across four minor versions, so this should be scoped as\n  its own dependency-bump PR before the `DecoratorMetadata`-specific plugin\n  audit. Blast radius is scoped to Pages Router / custom-`.babelrc` users\n  going through `next-babel-loader`; App Router / default builds increasingly\n  route through `next-swc`, which is unaffected.\n- **webpack**: No risk \u2014 no code changes needed. Only latent risk is\n  cosmetic: webpack's `devDependencies` (`@babel/core`, `babel-loader`,\n  `@babel/preset-react`) will drift out of date relative to the ecosystem\n  over time, but that only affects webpack's own example/test fixtures, not\n  anything it ships.\n"
